{
  "Version": 2,
  "Name": "Gerken2003-four-cavity-33layer",
  "Source": "M. Gerken and D. A. B. Miller, \"Multilayer thin-film structures with high spatial dispersion,\" Appl. Opt. 42, 1330-1345 (2003). doi:10.1364/AO.42.001330 - Appendix D, Table 1, 'Section 5' column (table layers 2-34); Sec. 5 text p. 1338; Fig. 8",
  "Notes": "Coupled-cavity all-pass (Gires-Tournois type) SiO2/Ta2O5 structure with four Ta2O5 cavities giving high narrowband spatial dispersion (stored energy)\nSpecification: Linear spatial shift with wavelength over a narrow range around 842-854 nm, s polarization, 54 deg; reflectance 100 %; optical cavity round-trip length 5.8 um (physical cavity ~2.6 um each)\nReported: Total thickness 15.4 um (sum of the table = 15431.9 nm). Fig. 8(b) (read from plot): shift ~0 at 842 nm, ~5.5 um at 846 nm, ~15.5 um at 850 nm, ~22.5 um at 854 nm. Reflectance 100 % (lossless).\nNotes: Table 1 lists layers from the substrate side (layer 1 = Ta2O5 next to the substrate; for this design layer 1 is blank, so the stack starts with SiO2 at table layer 2). Light is incident from the substrate side (Fig. 3: 'incident on the dielectric stack through the substrate'; Fig. 8(c): light from the left, cavities near the entrance, Bragg reflector at the far end), so the table order is kept as-is with the quartz substrate (n = 1.52) as the incident medium; exit medium assumed air (not stated). angle_deg = 54 is the incidence angle in vacuum/air (Eq. 9 convention); inside the quartz it is asin(sin54/1.52) = 32.2 deg. Indices n = 2.06 / 1.456 (Sec. 2, at 880 nm) assumed nondispersive.\nLayers from Appendix D, Table 1, 'Section 5' column (table layers 2-34); Sec. 5 text p. 1338; Fig. 8; layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nChecked with ThinFilmDesigner (2026-10-21), agrees with the paper: ours total 15431.9 nm; paper table sum 15431.9 nm (paper: 15.4 um).",
